(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["reliability",{"c:token-economics-and-unit-costs":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A per-token price tells you what one API call costs. The business cares about what one \u003cb>completed task\u003c/b> costs: a resolved ticket, a processed invoice, a merged pull request. Unit economics means adding up every model call, retry, guardrail check, piece of infrastructure and human hand-off behind a task, then dividing by the tasks that actually succeeded.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>From cost per call to cost per task\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The Billing module showed how to price a single request from its usage object. A real feature makes many calls per task: a router, the main model (several turns), guardrails, embeddings, sometimes a judge, plus retries. Some attempts fail and fall back to a person. The formulas teams use:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>AI cost per attempt   = all model calls + retries + guardrails + embeddings + (fixed infra / attempts)\nAI cost per success   = AI cost per attempt / success rate\nblended cost per task = AI cost per attempt + (1 - success rate) × cost of the fallback path\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three corrections beginners miss:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Divide by the success rate.\u003c/b> A conversation that ends in \"let me get a human\" still costs money.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Count every call in the attempt\u003c/b>, not just the main model: classifier, guardrails, query rewriting, embeddings, judge, tool APIs, retries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spread fixed costs\u003c/b> (vector database, tracing, hosting, the gateway) over volume. At low volume they often dominate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Worked example: a support assistant\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Illustrative prices, roughly the shape of late-2026 price sheets: the main mid-sized model costs $1 per million input tokens and $5 per million output tokens, with cache reads at a tenth of the input price; a small guardrail model costs $0.10 in and $0.50 out. One average conversation (four turns) looks like this:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Item\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Main model, uncached input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.0100\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Main model, cached input (shared prefix re-read each turn)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.0014\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Main model, output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,200\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.0060\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two guardrail checks on the small model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3,000 in, 40 out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.0003\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Query embeddings\u003c/td>\u003ctd>200\u003c/td>\u003ctd>under $0.0001\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retries (about 3% of main calls)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.0005\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Infrastructure: $1,500 a month over 100,000 conversations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.0150\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Total per conversation\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>$0.0332\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If 60% of conversations end resolved without a person:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>AI cost per resolved ticket = 0.0332 / 0.60          = $0.055\nblended cost per ticket     = 0.0332 + 0.40 × $6.00  = $2.43   (human handling costs $6)\nall-human baseline          = $6.00\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>What the numbers teach:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Infrastructure is almost half the AI bill here.\u003c/b> Model prices are not the only line; at small scale, fixed costs matter more.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Resolution rate is the biggest lever by far.\u003c/b> Raising it from 60% to 70% saves 0.10 × $6 = $0.60 per ticket, about 18 times the entire AI cost of a conversation. A more expensive model that resolves more tickets can lower the cost per task.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Output tokens are dear.\u003c/b> 1,200 output tokens cost 60% as much as 10,000 uncached input tokens.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Measuring it in production\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You cannot compute unit economics from the provider invoice. You need per-call records joined to outcomes:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>Tag every model call with \u003ccode>feature\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>task_id\u003c/code> (conversation or job), \u003ccode>tenant\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>model\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>attempt\u003c/code> and the usage fields, at the gateway or in your client.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Emit outcome events: resolved, escalated, abandoned, thumbs down.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Join them in your warehouse or observability tool (Langfuse, Phoenix, LangSmith and most gateways report cost per trace) and compute cost per task, cost per successful task, and the distribution.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Watch the \u003cb>heavy tail\u003c/b>. Cost per task is skewed: the top 1% of conversations (long, looping, many retries) often carries a large share of spend. Report p50, p95 and the top tasks, not just the mean.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-tip\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Tip\u003c/p>\u003cp>Agree a target with the business before launch, for example \"AI cost per resolved ticket under 5% of human cost\", and alert when the weekly value drifts up by more than 20%. Prompt, model and retrieval changes all move it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you compute the cost per resolved support ticket? Work it through for a bot that spends $0.0332 per conversation (all calls, retries and infrastructure included), resolves 60% of conversations, and hands the rest to people at $6 each.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Add up every cost behind one conversation (main model input, cached input and output, guardrails, embeddings, retries, and fixed infrastructure divided by volume), then divide by the success rate: $0.0332 / 0.60 ≈ $0.055 per resolved ticket. The number the business compares with the old process is the blended cost per ticket, which adds the human path for failures: $0.0332 + 0.40 × $6 = $2.43, against $6.00 if people handle everything.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In that example, why might switching to a model that costs twice as much per token reduce the cost per ticket?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model bill is about $0.02 per conversation, while each point of resolution rate is worth 0.01 × $6 = $0.06 per ticket. If the better model resolves even one or two more percentage points of conversations, the saved human time outweighs the doubled token cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three costs that teams forget when they estimate from token prices alone.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any three of: guardrail and router calls, embeddings and reranking, judge or evaluation calls, retries and failed attempts, tool and API fees, fixed infrastructure such as the vector database, tracing and hosting, and the human cost of escalations.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:latency-time-to-first-token-versus-total-time":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Users feel two numbers: \u003cb>time to first token (TTFT)\u003c/b>, how long until something appears, and \u003cb>total time\u003c/b>, how long until the answer is complete. Total time is dominated by the number of output tokens; TTFT by everything that happens before the model starts writing. OpenAI's seven latency principles are a checklist for attacking both.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where the time goes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Draw a \u003cb>latency budget\u003c/b> before optimising anything: list every sequential step and its typical time, taken from traces. An example for a retrieval-augmented support bot:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Step\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Network and authentication\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Input guardrail (run in parallel with retrieval)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0 ms added\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Embed the query\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Vector search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>40 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rerank 50 candidates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>120 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Model time to first token (queueing plus prefill)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>600 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Time to first token\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>about 0.84 s\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Generate 300 tokens at 60 tokens per second\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5.0 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Total\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>about 5.8 s\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The rule of thumb that falls out:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>total time ≈ TTFT + output tokens × time per output token\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Halving the output from 300 to 150 tokens cuts total time from 5.8 s to 3.3 s. Halving the prompt barely moves it, because input tokens are processed in parallel during prefill while output tokens are produced one at a time (the Inference mechanics module explains why).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The seven principles\u003c/h4>\u003cp>OpenAI's latency guide groups techniques into seven principles. They apply to any provider and to self-hosted models.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Principle\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it means\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical moves\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1. Process tokens faster\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Use a model or setup that generates faster\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Smaller or fine-tuned model for the step; lower reasoning effort; faster hardware or a priority tier\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2. Generate fewer tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output is the slowest part\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Answer in under 80 words\"; short JSON keys; a sensible \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>; ask for a diff, not the whole file\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3. Use fewer input tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only matters for very large contexts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prune retrieved chunks; strip HTML; keep a stable prefix so prompt caching skips prefill\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4. Make fewer requests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each round trip adds latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Merge sequential steps (rewrite query and decide whether to retrieve) into one call returning JSON fields\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>5. Parallelise\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Independent steps need not wait for each other\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run guardrails, retrieval and lookups concurrently; speculative execution\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>6. Make users wait less\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Perceived speed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stream tokens, stream progress steps, show sources early\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>7. Don't default to an LLM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Some steps need no model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hard-code confirmations, precompute frequent answers, use plain code or UI components\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The guide's own heuristics are worth remembering: cutting 50% of output tokens may cut about 50% of latency, while cutting 50% of the prompt may only improve latency by 1 to 5% unless the context is huge.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Speculative execution\u003c/b> (principle 5) deserves a word. If step 2 usually follows step 1, start both at once and cancel step 2 if step 1 comes back unexpectedly. Example: start generating the answer while the input moderation check runs; if moderation flags the message, discard the draft. When the guess is right, which is almost always, moderation costs zero added latency.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reasoning models add a twist: hidden thinking tokens are generated before the first visible token, so they inflate TTFT. Lower the reasoning effort for steps that do not need it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Measure the right things\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Record TTFT and total time separately, per step, as spans in a trace (the Observability module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Report \u003cb>p50 and p95\u003c/b> (and p99 for high-traffic paths), never only the mean. Tail latency comes from queueing under load, unusually long prompts or outputs, retries and provider variance.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Set targets per feature, for example \"TTFT p95 under 1.5 s, total p95 under 8 s\", and check them in load tests.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>A small helper to measure both numbers for a streamed call:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef timed(**request):\n    t0, ttft, out_tokens = time.perf_counter(), None, None\n    stream = client.chat.completions.create(stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}, **request)\n    for chunk in stream:\n        if ttft is None and chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n            ttft = time.perf_counter() - t0\n        if chunk.usage:\n            out_tokens = chunk.usage.completion_tokens\n    total = time.perf_counter() - t0\n    return {\"ttft_s\": ttft, \"total_s\": total, \"output_tokens\": out_tokens}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Apply three latency principles to a pipeline: a RAG chatbot takes about 0.8 s to its first token and 5.8 s in total, generating 300-token answers. Which three would you apply first and what gain would you expect?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(1) Make users wait less: stream the answer, so users start reading after about 0.8 s instead of 5.8 s. (2) Generate fewer tokens: ask for answers under 150 tokens, which cuts total time to about 3.3 s because generation time scales with output length. (3) Parallelise and make fewer requests: run the guardrail concurrently with retrieval and merge query rewriting into a single call, saving a few hundred milliseconds of TTFT; a smaller model for the reranker or rewriter (process tokens faster) helps too. Measure p50 and p95 before and after in traces.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does halving the prompt barely change latency while halving the output nearly halves it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prompt tokens are processed in parallel in one prefill pass, which is fast; output tokens are generated one after another, each needing a full pass over the model weights, so total time grows almost linearly with output length.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is speculative execution in an application pipeline? Give an example.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Starting a step before you know it is needed, betting on the likely outcome of an earlier step, and cancelling it if the bet is wrong. Example: generate the answer while moderation runs and discard it if moderation flags the input.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:streaming-for-perceived-speed":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Streaming does not make the model faster. It shows the answer while it is being produced, so the user starts reading after the time to first token instead of after the whole generation. For multi-step and agentic features, stream \u003cb>progress\u003c/b> too (\"searching 3 sources\", \"running the query\"), because a visible step feels far shorter than a spinner.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The architecture\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Most products stream from the model API to their own backend, then from the backend to the browser with \u003cb>server-sent events (SSE)\u003c/b>: a long-lived HTTP response with \u003ccode>Content-Type: text/event-stream\u003c/code>, where each message is a few \u003ccode>field: value\u003c/code> lines followed by a blank line.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Browser\u003c/b>\u003csmall>EventSource or fetch stream\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Backend\u003c/b>\u003csmall>SSE endpoint\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model API\u003c/b>\u003csmall>stream=True\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model API\u003c/b>\u003csmall>token deltas\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Backend\u003c/b>\u003csmall>forward deltas, add status and usage events\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Browser\u003c/b>\u003csmall>render as they arrive\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Backend: forward tokens and progress\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A FastAPI endpoint that emits three kinds of named events: \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>token\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>done\u003c/code>, plus \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> for cost logging.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\napp = FastAPI()\nclient = AsyncOpenAI()\n\ndef sse(event: str, data: dict) -&gt; str:\n    return f\"event: {event}\\ndata: {json.dumps(data)}\\n\\n\"\n\n@app.get(\"/ask\")\nasync def ask(q: str):\n    async def events():\n        yield sse(\"status\", {\"text\": \"Searching the help centre...\"})\n        docs = await search(q)                      # your retrieval\n        yield sse(\"status\", {\"text\": f\"Found {len(docs)} articles\"})\n        yield sse(\"sources\", {\"ids\": [d.id for d in docs]})\n        stream = await client.chat.completions.create(\n            model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True},\n            messages=build_messages(q, docs))\n        async for chunk in stream:\n            if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n                yield sse(\"token\", {\"text\": chunk.choices[0].delta.content})\n            if chunk.usage:\n                yield sse(\"usage\", chunk.usage.model_dump())\n        yield sse(\"done\", {})\n    return StreamingResponse(events(), media_type=\"text/event-stream\",\n                             headers={\"Cache-Control\": \"no-cache\", \"X-Accel-Buffering\": \"no\"})\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With \u003ccode>include_usage\u003c/code>, the last chunk has an empty \u003ccode>choices\u003c/code> list and a \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> object, which is why the code checks both.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Browser: render as events arrive\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">javascript\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>const es = new EventSource(`/ask?q=${encodeURIComponent(question)}`);\nes.addEventListener(\"status\", (e) =&gt; showStatus(JSON.parse(e.data).text));\nes.addEventListener(\"sources\", (e) =&gt; showSources(JSON.parse(e.data).ids));\nes.addEventListener(\"token\", (e) =&gt; appendText(JSON.parse(e.data).text));\nes.addEventListener(\"done\", () =&gt; es.close());\nes.onerror = () =&gt; es.close();\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>EventSource\u003c/code> only does GET requests without custom headers, so apps that need POST bodies or an \u003ccode>Authorization\u003c/code> header use \u003ccode>fetch()\u003c/code> and read \u003ccode>response.body\u003c/code> as a stream instead; the SSE text format stays the same.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What to stream besides text\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Status steps\u003c/b> for agents and pipelines: tool names, \"querying orders\", \"reading 4 files\". Real progress, not fake percentages.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sources\u003c/b> as soon as retrieval finishes, so users can start checking them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Partial structured output\u003c/b>: stream JSON and parse it with a tolerant partial-JSON parser, or stream fields as separate events.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Usage\u003c/b> at the end, so the backend can log cost per request without a second call.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Pitfalls that break streaming in production\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Buffering proxies.\u003c/b> Nginx, some load balancers, compression middleware and certain serverless platforms buffer the response, so everything arrives at once. Send \u003ccode>X-Accel-Buffering: no\u003c/code> (or set \u003ccode>proxy_buffering off\u003c/code>), disable compression on the stream route, and test through the real production path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Auto-reconnect.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>EventSource\u003c/code> reconnects automatically when a connection drops. If your endpoint starts a new generation on every connection, a flaky network can trigger several paid generations. Close the source on \u003ccode>done\u003c/code>, and make the endpoint resumable or idempotent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Client disconnects.\u003c/b> When the user closes the tab, stop the upstream model call (cancel the task or close the stream). Closing the upstream connection usually stops generation, so you stop paying for tokens nobody will read.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Errors after the first byte.\u003c/b> Once streaming starts, the HTTP status is already 200. Send an explicit \u003ccode>error\u003c/code> event and let the UI show it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Output moderation.\u003c/b> You cannot check an answer you have already shown. Moderate in chunks (per sentence or paragraph) with a small display delay, and be ready to retract.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Timeouts.\u003c/b> Use an idle timeout between chunks as well as a total deadline; a stream that stalls for 30 seconds is broken even if the total limit is 10 minutes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-note\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Note\u003c/p>\u003cp>Measure TTFT where users feel it: in the browser, from click to first rendered token. Server-side TTFT hides queueing, proxies and rendering.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you stream partial results and progress updates from an LLM pipeline to a web UI?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Call the model with \u003ccode>stream=True\u003c/code> on the backend and expose your own SSE endpoint (\u003ccode>text/event-stream\u003c/code>) that forwards each token delta as an event, interleaved with named status events for pipeline steps (\"searching\", \"found 4 sources\", tool names), a sources event once retrieval finishes, a usage event at the end and a done event. The browser listens with \u003ccode>EventSource\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>fetch\u003c/code> with a stream reader for POST and auth headers) and appends text as it arrives. Disable proxy buffering, stop the upstream call when the client disconnects, and send an explicit error event if something fails mid-stream.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Streaming works on your laptop, but in production the whole answer appears at once after ten seconds. What is the likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Something between the server and the browser is buffering the response, typically a reverse proxy such as Nginx, a load balancer, compression middleware or the hosting platform. Disable buffering for that route (for example \u003ccode>X-Accel-Buffering: no\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>proxy_buffering off\u003c/code>) and turn off compression for the stream.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can EventSource's automatic reconnection cost you money?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If the connection drops before you close it, the browser reconnects and calls your endpoint again, which may start a brand-new paid generation each time. Close the source when the \u003ccode>done\u003c/code> event arrives and make the endpoint resume or deduplicate requests.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:caching-prompt-caching-and-response-caching":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Three different things are called \"caching\". \u003cb>Prompt caching\u003c/b> reuses the model's computed state for an identical prompt prefix; the model still writes a fresh answer. An \u003cb>exact response cache\u003c/b> returns a stored answer for an identical request, so no model call happens. A \u003cb>semantic cache\u003c/b> returns a stored answer for a \u003ci>similar\u003c/i> request. Each step up saves more and risks more.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The three layers compared\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Prompt (prefix) cache\u003c/th>\u003cth>Exact response cache\u003c/th>\u003cth>Semantic cache\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lives in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider or inference server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your gateway or app (Redis, disk)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your gateway or app (vector index)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reuses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Attention state (KV cache) of a prefix\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The full response\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The response to a near-duplicate question\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hit needs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Byte-identical prefix above a minimum length, within the TTL\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Identical model, messages, tools and parameters\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Embedding similarity above a threshold\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Saves\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most of the input cost and prefill time; output is still generated and paid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The whole call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The whole call\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Correctness risk\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stale answers; cross-user leaks if the key is not scoped\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong answers to questions that only look alike\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Good fit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long shared system prompts, tool lists, multi-turn chats, agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Repeated jobs, retries, idempotent steps, popular fixed queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anonymous single-turn FAQ traffic with stable answers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Prompt caching is the one you should almost always have: put stable content first and append history (the Billing module covers the provider rules). The other two are application decisions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>An exact-match response cache\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The key must contain everything that can change the answer, including your prompt version and the tenant, and nothing that does not (such as request ids).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import hashlib, json\nimport redis\n\nr = redis.Redis()\n\ndef cache_key(request: dict, prompt_version: str, tenant: str) -&gt; str:\n    canon = json.dumps({\"v\": prompt_version, \"tenant\": tenant, **request},\n                       sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"))\n    return \"llm:resp:\" + hashlib.sha256(canon.encode()).hexdigest()\n\ndef cached_completion(client, request: dict, prompt_version: str, tenant: str, ttl_s=86_400):\n    key = cache_key(request, prompt_version, tenant)\n    if (hit := r.get(key)) is not None:\n        r.incr(\"llm:stats:hit\")\n        return json.loads(hit)\n    r.incr(\"llm:stats:miss\")\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(**request).model_dump()\n    r.set(key, json.dumps(response), ex=ttl_s)\n    return response\n\ndef hit_rate() -&gt; float:\n    hits, misses = int(r.get(\"llm:stats:hit\") or 0), int(r.get(\"llm:stats:miss\") or 0)\n    return hits / (hits + misses) if hits + misses else 0.0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Rules that keep it safe:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Only cache what may be reused.\u003c/b> Temperature-0 classification, extraction, query rewriting, embeddings, retries of the same job. Not creative writing where users expect variety, and never calls whose tool results have side effects.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scope by tenant or user\u003c/b> whenever answers can contain private data.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Version the key.\u003c/b> Putting \u003ccode>prompt_version\u003c/code> and the model in the key invalidates old entries automatically when either changes. A TTL handles content that goes stale.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure per feature.\u003c/b> Hit rate = hits / (hits + misses). Log hit or miss on every request, so you can also see the latency and cost saved.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Exact hit rates are usually modest for free-text chat, because users rarely type identical messages and any change in history is a miss. They can be high for pipelines that re-run the same inputs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Semantic caching\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Question\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Embed\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Nearest cached question\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Similarity at or above threshold?\u003c/b>\u003csmall>return cached answer\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Otherwise\u003c/b>\u003csmall>call model, store question and answer\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The threshold is the whole game. \"How do I reset my password?\" and \"How do I reset my 2FA device?\" share most of their words and may embed close together, but need different answers. A lower threshold catches more paraphrases and serves more wrong answers. LiteLLM's documentation warns that semantic caching is designed for single-shot prompts and will replay stale responses on multi-turn or agentic traffic, where the same last message means different things in different conversations.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Do the arithmetic before enabling it. Suppose 100,000 questions a day cost $0.0055 each ($550 a day). An exact cache with an 18% hit rate brings that to $451. A semantic layer adding another 12% of hits brings it to $385, saving $66 a day. If 2% of those semantic hits are wrong, that is 240 wrong answers a day. Whether that trade is acceptable depends on what a wrong answer costs you; for support or medical content it usually is not. (The hit rates here are an example; measure your own in the lab.)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Safer uses of semantic matching: suggest a cached answer to a human agent, or use it to route to a canned workflow, rather than silently answering.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Other caches worth having\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tool and retrieval results\u003c/b>: search results, API lookups and database reads with a TTL. These often cost more latency than the model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Embeddings\u003c/b>: key by a hash of the text and the embedding model, so re-indexing unchanged documents is free.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Precomputed answers\u003c/b> for the top questions, generated offline (often with the Batch API) and reviewed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Tools: gateways such as LiteLLM (Redis, Redis or Valkey semantic, Qdrant semantic, S3), Portkey and Cloudflare AI Gateway offer caching built in; libraries such as RedisVL's semantic cache and GPTCache do it in-process.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you add an exact-match response cache to an LLM feature and measure its hit rate?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Build a key by hashing a canonical JSON of everything that affects the answer (model, messages, tools, sampling parameters, prompt version and tenant), look it up in Redis before calling the model, and on a miss call the model and store the response with a TTL. Count hits and misses per feature on every request; hit rate is hits divided by hits plus misses. Only cache deterministic, side-effect-free calls, scope keys by tenant when answers can contain private data, and log the latency and cost saved by hits.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between prompt caching and response caching?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prompt caching reuses the provider's computed attention state for an identical prefix, cutting input cost and time to first token, but the model still generates a new answer and you pay for its output. Response caching skips the model call entirely and returns a stored answer, which saves everything but is only correct when the stored answer still applies.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When is a semantic cache dangerous, and what would you do instead?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When questions that look alike need different answers (password versus 2FA reset), when answers are personalised or time-sensitive, and in multi-turn or agentic traffic where the same last message depends on earlier context. Use exact caching plus prompt caching instead, or use semantic matches only as suggestions for a human or to route to a reviewed canned answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:batch-and-flex-processing":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Work that does not need an answer within seconds should not pay interactive prices. \u003cb>Batch APIs\u003c/b> take a file of requests and return results within 24 hours at about half price. \u003cb>Flex\u003c/b> tiers keep the normal request shape but accept slower responses and occasional \"no capacity\" errors for a similar discount. Moving evals, enrichment and backfills off the interactive tier is often the easiest 50% saving in a company.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Which work can wait\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Workload\u003c/th>\u003cth>How fast it must finish\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tier\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Chat, autocomplete, agent steps a user is watching\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Standard, or a priority tier for strict latency\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Eval runs in CI, nightly regression suites\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minutes to hours\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Flex, or batch for big suites\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Document tagging, summaries, enrichment, metadata extraction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hours\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Batch\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Backfills, re-processing a corpus, synthetic data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A day\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Batch\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Background jobs triggered by webhooks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minutes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Flex, falling back to standard\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The options (as of late 2026; check current docs)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Provider\u003c/th>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>Price\u003c/th>\u003cth>Behaviour\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Batch API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50% off\u003c/td>\u003ctd>JSONL file of requests with a \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code> each; one model per file; completes within 24 hours; its own, higher rate limits\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Flex processing (\u003ccode>service_tier: \"flex\"\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Batch rates, plus prompt-caching discounts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Same API call; slower; may return 429 \"resource unavailable\", which is not charged; beta, limited models\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Anthropic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Message Batches\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50% off\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB per batch; most finish within an hour; expire after 24 hours; results kept 29 days\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Google Gemini\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Batch mode\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50% off\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Target turnaround 24 hours, usually faster\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Providers also sell the opposite: OpenAI's Fast mode (formerly priority processing) charges more per token for faster, steadier responses. Use it on the latency-critical path only.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A nightly job enriches 200,000 documents, each 1,500 input and 200 output tokens, on a model priced at $1 in and $5 out per million tokens:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>standard: 200,000 × (1,500 × $1 + 200 × $5) / 1,000,000 = $500 per night\nbatch:    50% of that                                    = $250 per night\nsaving:   $250 × 30 nights                               = $7,500 per month\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Submitting a batch (OpenAI shape)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\nwith open(\"batch.jsonl\", \"w\") as f:\n    for doc in docs:\n        f.write(json.dumps({\n            \"custom_id\": doc[\"id\"],                  # stable id, used to join results\n            \"method\": \"POST\", \"url\": \"/v1/chat/completions\",\n            \"body\": {\"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\", \"messages\": [\n                {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": TAG_PROMPT},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": doc[\"text\"]}]},\n        }) + \"\\n\")\n\nupload = client.files.create(file=open(\"batch.jsonl\", \"rb\"), purpose=\"batch\")\nbatch = client.batches.create(input_file_id=upload.id, endpoint=\"/v1/chat/completions\",\n                              completion_window=\"24h\")\n\n# Later, from a scheduled job:\nbatch = client.batches.retrieve(batch.id)\nif batch.status == \"completed\":\n    lines = client.files.content(batch.output_file_id).text.splitlines()\n    results = {r[\"custom_id\"]: r[\"response\"][\"body\"] for r in map(json.loads, lines)}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Output lines are not guaranteed to be in input order, so always join on \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>. Failed requests appear in a separate error file (\u003ccode>error_file_id\u003c/code>); re-submit only those.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Flex with a fallback\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI, RateLimitError\n\nclient = OpenAI(timeout=900.0)        # flex calls can be slow; the SDK default is 10 minutes\n\ndef flex_or_standard(**request):\n    try:\n        return client.chat.completions.create(service_tier=\"flex\", **request)\n    except RateLimitError:            # includes flex's 429 \"resource unavailable\"\n        return client.chat.completions.create(service_tier=\"auto\", **request)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If the job can wait, retry flex with exponential backoff instead of paying standard prices.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Design for partial failure.\u003c/b> Make the job idempotent: stable \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code> values, results written by id, a re-run that only submits missing ids.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expiry.\u003c/b> A batch that does not finish within the window expires; unfinished requests must be resubmitted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rate limits still exist\u003c/b>: batch queues have their own limits on tokens waiting to be processed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Do not batch what a user waits for\u003c/b>, and do not hide a 24-hour delay inside a feature that promises \"minutes\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Combine discounts.\u003c/b> A batch job with a long shared prompt prefix can also benefit from prompt caching where the provider supports it; check the docs for how the discounts stack.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you move an offline job, such as nightly document tagging, to batch or flex pricing?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For batch: write one JSONL line per document with a stable \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>, method, URL and the usual request body; upload it with purpose \u003ccode>batch\u003c/code>; create the batch with a 24-hour completion window; poll from a scheduled job; download the output file, join results by \u003ccode>custom_id\u003c/code>, and resubmit only ids in the error file or missing after expiry. For flex: keep the normal call, add \u003ccode>service_tier=\"flex\"\u003c/code>, raise the client timeout, and handle 429 \"resource unavailable\" by backing off or retrying on the standard tier. Either way the job should be idempotent so re-runs are safe.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you choose flex over batch?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the code is simpler as normal synchronous calls (for example an eval harness or a webhook worker), the work should finish in minutes rather than up to a day, and you can tolerate slower responses and occasional capacity errors. Batch suits large offline jobs that can wait hours.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does a nightly job of 50,000 requests at $0.004 each cost per month on standard pricing, and on batch?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>50,000 × $0.004 = $200 a night, about $6,000 a month; at 50% off, $100 a night or about $3,000 a month.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:model-routing-and-cascades":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Most traffic is easy, and easy requests do not need the most expensive model. A \u003cb>router\u003c/b> decides up front which model should handle a request; a \u003cb>cascade\u003c/b> tries a cheap model first and escalates to a stronger one when a check fails. Both trade some engineering and a little risk for large savings, and both must be validated with an eval rather than assumed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Router versus cascade\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Router\u003c/b>\u003csmall>rules or small classifier\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Small model or large model\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Small model\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Check\u003c/b>\u003csmall>validator or confidence\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Pass\u003c/b>\u003csmall>return answer\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Fail\u003c/b>\u003csmall>escalate to large model\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Router\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cascade\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Decision made\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Before any answer exists\u003c/td>\u003ctd>After seeing the cheap answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Router call plus one model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small model always, plus large model on escalation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One model call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Escalated requests pay for two calls\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Best when\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Difficulty is predictable from the input (type, length, user tier)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Answers can be checked (schema, tests, citations)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Research supports both. FrugalGPT (2023) reported cascades that matched the best single model with up to 98% lower cost on its benchmarks; RouteLLM (2024) trained routers on human preference data and cut cost by more than two times in some settings without hurting response quality. Your numbers will differ: measure on your own eval.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The cost maths\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With 3,000 input and 500 output tokens per request, a small model at $0.10 / $0.50 per million costs $0.00055 per request and a large model at $4 / $20 costs $0.022:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>cascade cost = small + escalation rate × large\n15% escalated: 0.00055 + 0.15 × 0.022 = $0.0039  (82% cheaper than always-large)\n25% escalated: 0.00055 + 0.25 × 0.022 = $0.0061  (72% cheaper)\n40% escalated: 0.00055 + 0.40 × 0.022 = $0.0094  (57% cheaper)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The saving shrinks as escalation rises, and it is only real if quality holds on the requests the small model keeps.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Confidence signals, from strongest to weakest\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Verifiable checks\u003c/b> on the actual output: JSON validates against the schema, code compiles and tests pass, SQL runs, every citation points at a retrieved passage, the label is one of the allowed values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Token log-probabilities\u003c/b> of a short answer, such as a one-letter class label, where the API returns them (\u003ccode>logprobs=True\u003c/code>). Reasoning models often do not expose them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A verifier or judge model\u003c/b> that grades the cheap answer. Effective but adds a call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Self-reported confidence\u003c/b> (\"rate your confidence from 1 to 10\"). Poorly calibrated: models say 9 when wrong. Never use it alone.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Rules are signals too: route by feature, customer tier, input length, attachments or language.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A classifier router with a confidence threshold\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nROUTER_PROMPT = (\"Classify the customer question. Reply with one letter: S if one help-centre \"\n                 \"article answers it, C if it needs several steps, account data or judgement.\")\n\ndef choose_model(question: str, threshold: float = 0.85) -&gt; str:\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"&lt;small-model&gt;\", temperature=0, max_completion_tokens=1,\n        logprobs=True, top_logprobs=2,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": ROUTER_PROMPT},\n                  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}])\n    top = r.choices[0].logprobs.content[0]\n    label, p = top.token.strip().upper(), math.exp(top.logprob)\n    if label == \"S\" and p &gt;= threshold:\n        return \"&lt;small-model&gt;\"\n    return \"&lt;large-model&gt;\"        # complex, or not confident enough to risk it\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Single-letter labels keep the answer to one token, so its probability is meaningful. (Some OpenAI-compatible servers expect \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>max_completion_tokens\u003c/code>.) A cascade on top checks the small model's draft:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def answer(question, sources):\n    model = choose_model(question)\n    draft = generate(model, question, sources)\n    if model == \"&lt;small-model&gt;\" and not passes_checks(draft, sources):\n        draft = generate(\"&lt;large-model&gt;\", question, sources)    # escalate\n    return draft\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Validating a router\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Calibrate the threshold on labelled data.\u003c/b> For each threshold, measure accuracy on the requests kept by the small model and the escalation rate; pick the cheapest point that meets your quality bar.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Look for silent failures\u003c/b>: wrong answers from the small model that pass every check. They are the real cost of routing; sample and review them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Monitor drift.\u003c/b> Track the escalation rate daily. New kinds of questions or a model update can move it sharply.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-tune when models change.\u003c/b> A new small model may handle far more traffic.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you route requests with a cheap classifier and escalate on low confidence?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Ask a small, fast model to classify each request with a one-token label (for example S for simple, C for complex), read the label's log-probability, and send the request to the small model only if the label is simple and its probability exceeds a threshold; otherwise send it to the large model. Add a cascade check on the small model's answer (schema, citations, tests) that escalates failures. Calibrate the threshold on labelled examples so accuracy on small-model traffic meets your bar, and monitor the escalation rate and sampled silent failures in production.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Using the prices above, what does a cascade cost per request if 30% of requests escalate?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>$0.00055 + 0.30 × $0.022 = $0.00715 per request, about 68% cheaper than sending everything to the large model at $0.022.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is a model's self-reported confidence a weak routing signal?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Models are not calibrated when they describe their own certainty in words; they often state high confidence while wrong. Checks on the output, log-probabilities of a short label, or a separate verifier correlate much better with correctness.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:fallbacks-retries-and-provider-redundancy":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Model providers have outages, overload periods and rate limits. A resilient system \u003cb>classifies\u003c/b> each error, \u003cb>retries\u003c/b> the retryable ones a bounded number of times with backoff and jitter, then \u003cb>fails over\u003c/b> to another deployment, provider or model, and stops sending traffic to a sick provider for a while (a \u003cb>circuit breaker\u003c/b>). All of this should happen in exactly one layer.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Classify the error first\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Error\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>Retry?\u003c/th>\u003cth>Fail over?\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Timeout or connection reset\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No first token after 10 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, once or twice\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>429 rate limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your tokens-per-minute are used up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>After \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, to another key, region or provider\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>429 ramp limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI \u003ccode>slow_down\u003c/code>: traffic rose too quickly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Follow \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code>, then slow down\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Maybe\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>500, 502, 503, 529 overloaded\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider trouble\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, with backoff\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>400, 422 invalid request\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bad parameters or schema\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No: it is your bug\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>401, 403\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bad or revoked key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No: alert someone\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Context length exceeded\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompt too long for this model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>To a longer-context model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Content-policy refusal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider filter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only if policy allows another model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Retrying a 400 just repeats the failure, and failing over a 401 hides a security problem.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Backoff, jitter and the retry budget\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Wait \u003ccode>random(0, base × 2^attempt)\u003c/code> seconds between attempts (\u003cb>exponential backoff with full jitter\u003c/b>), cap the wait, and honour \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> when present. Jitter stops thousands of clients retrying in lockstep.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The classic failure is \u003cb>retry amplification\u003c/b>. If the browser, your API, your gateway and the SDK each retry, attempts multiply: three layers that each make 4 attempts (1 plus 3 retries) send up to 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 calls per user request, exactly when the provider is struggling. Retry in \u003cb>one\u003c/b> layer, usually the gateway; set \u003ccode>max_retries=0\u003c/code> in SDK clients behind it; cap retries at a small share of traffic (a retry budget, for example 10%).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Timeouts per phase\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One big timeout is not enough. Use a connect timeout (a few seconds), a \u003cb>time-to-first-token timeout\u003c/b> for streams (for example 10 to 20 s, longer for reasoning models), an \u003cb>idle timeout\u003c/b> between chunks, and an overall deadline derived from what the user will tolerate. A stuck stream should fail over quickly instead of hanging for ten minutes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Fallback chains and circuit breakers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A typical chain, cheapest to most disruptive:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>The same model in another deployment or region (Azure OpenAI, Amazon Bedrock and Google Vertex AI host several vendors' models).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The same model from another provider.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A different model of similar quality, with its own tested prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A smaller model with reduced features, or a cached or static answer (\"search is slow right now, here are the top articles\").\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>A \u003cb>circuit breaker\u003c/b> marks a deployment as unhealthy after a few failures in a window and skips it for a cooldown period, so you stop paying timeouts to a dead endpoint. LiteLLM calls these \u003ccode>allowed_fails\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>cooldown_time\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Availability maths\u003c/b>: two independent providers at 99.5% each give 1 − 0.005² = 99.9975%, cutting expected downtime from about 216 minutes to about 1 minute a month. Real failures are correlated (same cloud region, same upstream model, shared DNS), so the gain is smaller; test it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code: retry, then fail over\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random, time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nimport openai\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nPRIMARY = OpenAI(base_url=\"https://provider-a.example/v1\", api_key=KEY_A, timeout=30, max_retries=0)\nSECONDARY = OpenAI(base_url=\"https://provider-b.example/v1\", api_key=KEY_B, timeout=30, max_retries=0)\nCHAIN = [(PRIMARY, \"&lt;model-a&gt;\"), (SECONDARY, \"&lt;model-b&gt;\")]\nRETRYABLE = (openai.APITimeoutError, openai.APIConnectionError,\n             openai.RateLimitError, openai.InternalServerError)\nFAILS = defaultdict(list)\n\ndef breaker_open(model, window=60, limit=3):\n    FAILS[model] = [t for t in FAILS[model] if time.monotonic() - t &lt; window]\n    return len(FAILS[model]) &gt;= limit\n\ndef backoff(err, attempt):\n    try:\n        return min(float(err.response.headers[\"retry-after\"]), 30)\n    except (AttributeError, KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):\n        return random.uniform(0, 0.5 * 2 ** attempt)        # full jitter\n\ndef complete(messages, attempts_per_provider=2):\n    last = None\n    for client, model in CHAIN:\n        if breaker_open(model):\n            continue\n        for attempt in range(attempts_per_provider):\n            try:\n                return client.chat.completions.create(model=model, messages=messages)\n            except RETRYABLE as err:                       # 400, 401 and friends propagate\n                last = err\n                FAILS[model].append(time.monotonic())\n                time.sleep(backoff(err, attempt))\n    raise last or RuntimeError(\"all providers are in cooldown\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same policy as LiteLLM proxy configuration:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">yaml\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>model_list:\n  - model_name: chat\n    litellm_params: {model: openai/&lt;model-a&gt;, api_key: os.environ/OPENAI_API_KEY}\n  - model_name: chat-backup\n    litellm_params: {model: anthropic/&lt;model-b&gt;, api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}\nlitellm_settings:\n  num_retries: 2\n  request_timeout: 30\n  fallbacks: [{\"chat\": [\"chat-backup\"]}]\n  context_window_fallbacks: [{\"chat\": [\"chat-backup\"]}]\nrouter_settings:\n  allowed_fails: 3\n  cooldown_time: 30\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Things that bite\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Side effects.\u003c/b> Never blindly retry a call that triggered a tool action (refund, email). Use idempotency keys so a repeat is harmless.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Untested fallbacks.\u003c/b> The backup model needs its own prompt and must pass your eval suite; run it in CI and in a scheduled synthetic check, or it will fail the day you need it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hedged requests\u003c/b> cut tail latency: if no first token arrives by your p95 TTFT, send a second request elsewhere and cancel the loser. They cost extra, so use them only on latency-critical paths.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you fail over to a second provider on errors and timeouts?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Wrap model calls in one layer (a gateway or client wrapper) that sets per-phase timeouts, disables SDK-level retries, classifies errors, retries only retryable ones (timeouts, connection errors, 429, 5xx) a bounded number of times with exponential backoff, full jitter and \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code>, and then moves to the next entry in a fallback chain: another deployment or region, another provider, or another tested model. A circuit breaker skips a provider after repeated failures for a cooldown period, and non-retryable errors such as 400 and 401 propagate immediately.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your browser client, API server and SDK each retry three times. What happens during a provider outage?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each layer makes up to four attempts, so a single user action can produce 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 calls to the struggling provider, increasing load, cost and latency exactly when it is failing. Retry in one layer and turn retries off elsewhere.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should a context-length error not be retried on the same model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The request will fail identically every time. Either shorten the input (trim history, fewer retrieved chunks) or send it to a model with a longer context window, which is what context-window fallbacks do.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:budgets-and-limits-for-agents":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An agent decides for itself how many model calls to make, and every call re-sends a context that keeps growing, so cost grows much faster than the number of steps. One confused task can loop for hundreds of steps and burn more than a day of normal traffic. Give every task hard budgets for steps, tokens, dollars and wall-clock time, check them \u003cb>before\u003c/b> each call, and decide in advance what happens when one runs out.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why agent costs explode\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each step sends the whole conversation so far: the system prompt, tool definitions, every earlier tool call and result. Suppose an agent starts at 8,000 tokens, each step adds 2,000 tokens of tool results, and each step writes 300 output tokens on a frontier model at $4 in and $20 out per million:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Steps\u003c/th>\u003cth>Total input tokens sent\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost without caching\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>170,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.74\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>30\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,110,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$4.62\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>100\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10,700,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$43.40\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>200\u003c/td>\u003ctd>41,400,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$166.80\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Input grows with the square of the number of steps, because step \u003ci>n\u003c/i> re-sends everything from steps 1 to \u003ci>n − 1\u003c/i>. Prompt caching softens this a lot (the 30-step run drops to roughly $0.93 when cache reads cost a tenth of the input price), but a runaway loop is still expensive, and caching does nothing for tools that cost money per call, such as web search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The limits to set\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Limit\u003c/th>\u003cth>Protects against\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>Where it lives\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Max steps (turns)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Infinite loops\u003c/td>\u003ctd>40\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agent loop: \u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code> in the OpenAI and Claude Agent SDKs, \u003ccode>recursion_limit\u003c/code> in LangGraph\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Max tokens per task\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Context explosion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.5 million input plus output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your wrapper around the model call\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Max dollars per task\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Expensive models and tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your wrapper; Claude Agent SDK \u003ccode>max_budget_usd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Wall-clock deadline\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hangs, slow tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10 minutes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Orchestrator or job runner\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Per-tool caps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Runaway searches, paid APIs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20 web searches\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tool layer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Max output per call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rambling generations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,000 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Request parameter\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Per-user or per-key budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Abuse, bugs at scale\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$20 a day\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Gateway (for example LiteLLM keys with \u003ccode>max_budget\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>budget_duration\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Framework defaults are not budgets. LangGraph's default \u003ccode>recursion_limit\u003c/code> is 1,000 steps (since version 1.0.6), which is far too generous for most tasks. The Claude Agent SDK documents that its cost total is a client-side estimate that can pass \u003ccode>max_budget_usd\u003c/code>, so leave headroom.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A budget object checked before every call\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nclass BudgetExceeded(Exception):\n    pass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Budget:\n    max_steps: int = 40\n    max_tokens: int = 1_500_000\n    max_usd: float = 3.00\n    deadline_s: float = 600\n    steps: int = 0\n    tokens: int = 0\n    usd: float = 0.0\n    start: float = field(default_factory=time.monotonic)\n\n    def check(self, next_input_tokens: int, price_in: float) -&gt; None:\n        \"\"\"Call before each model call; input size is known in advance.\"\"\"\n        if self.steps &gt;= self.max_steps:\n            raise BudgetExceeded(\"steps\")\n        if self.tokens + next_input_tokens &gt; self.max_tokens:\n            raise BudgetExceeded(\"tokens\")\n        if self.usd + next_input_tokens * price_in / 1e6 &gt; self.max_usd:\n            raise BudgetExceeded(\"dollars\")\n        if time.monotonic() - self.start &gt; self.deadline_s:\n            raise BudgetExceeded(\"time\")\n\n    def charge(self, usage, price_in: float, price_out: float) -&gt; None:\n        \"\"\"Call after each model call with the response's usage object.\"\"\"\n        self.steps += 1\n        self.tokens += usage.prompt_tokens + usage.completion_tokens\n        self.usd += (usage.prompt_tokens * price_in + usage.completion_tokens * price_out) / 1e6\n\n    def remaining_note(self) -&gt; str | None:\n        if self.steps &gt;= 0.8 * self.max_steps:\n            return f\"Budget note: {self.max_steps - self.steps} steps left. Wrap up and report progress.\"\n        return None\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In the loop: \u003ccode>budget.check(count_tokens(messages), PRICE_IN)\u003c/code> before the call, \u003ccode>budget.charge(response.usage, PRICE_IN, PRICE_OUT)\u003c/code> after it. Ignoring cache discounts makes the dollar check conservative, which is what you want from a limit. For multi-agent systems, give each subagent its own budget and charge it to the parent as well.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When a budget runs out\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Failing with a stack trace wastes everything the agent did. Better options:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Wrap up gracefully.\u003c/b> At 80% of the budget, tell the agent in its context how much is left so it converges (\"5 steps left, summarise and stop\"). At 100%, return the progress so far and what remains.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ask for more.\u003c/b> Pause and request approval from the user or an operator (the Graphs module shows human-in-the-loop interrupts).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Escalate\u003c/b> to a human queue with the trace attached.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Log it as a failure category.\u003c/b> Budget hits are a strong signal of loops, unhelpful tool results or impossible tasks; review them in error analysis.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Setting the numbers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start from traces of successful tasks: take the p95 of steps, tokens and cost and set caps at two to three times that. Add \u003cb>loop detection\u003c/b>: the same tool called with the same arguments three times in a row almost always means the agent is stuck. Review the caps monthly, and whenever you change model, prompts or tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you enforce per-task token and dollar budgets on an agent?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep a budget object per task that tracks steps, tokens, dollars and elapsed time. Before each model call, estimate the input tokens (known from the messages) and refuse the call if it would exceed any cap; after the call, charge the actual usage at the model's prices. Add per-tool caps and a per-user budget at the gateway, warn the agent near the limit so it wraps up, and on exhaustion return partial progress, ask for approval or escalate, and log the event for review. Set caps from the p95 of successful tasks, for example two to three times p95.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does an agent's input-token cost grow roughly with the square of the number of steps?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every step re-sends the whole history, which grows by a roughly constant amount per step, so the total sent over n steps is about n × start + increment × n²/2. Doubling the steps roughly quadruples the input tokens once history dominates.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">LangGraph's default recursion limit is 1,000. Why is relying on it a mistake?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is a safety net against infinite graphs, not a budget. A 1,000-step run with a growing context could cost hundreds of dollars and take hours before it stops. Set a limit that matches the task, plus token, dollar and time caps.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:reliability/llm-gateways-in-production":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An \u003cb>LLM gateway\u003c/b> is a reverse proxy that every model call in the organisation goes through. It offers one OpenAI-compatible API in front of many providers and adds the cross-cutting concerns: keys and budgets per team, routing, retries and fallbacks, caching, guardrail hooks, logging and cost attribution. It is where this module's techniques get implemented once instead of in every application.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The request path\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>App\u003c/b>\u003csmall>OpenAI SDK, model alias\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Gateway\u003c/b>\u003csmall>auth, budget and rate-limit check\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Policy\u003c/b>\u003csmall>cache lookup, routing, guardrails\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Provider A or B\u003c/b>\u003csmall>timeouts, retries, fallbacks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Gateway\u003c/b>\u003csmall>log usage, cost and trace\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>App\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What a gateway is responsible for\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Concern\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gateway feature\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Access\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Virtual keys per team or app, model allow-lists, SSO for the admin UI\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Spend tracking by key, team, user and tag; budgets with reset windows; alerts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reliability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retries, fallbacks, cooldowns, load balancing across deployments, regions and keys\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Performance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Response caching, streaming passthrough, sticky routing that keeps a conversation on one deployment so prompt caches stay warm\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Safety\u003c/td>\u003ctd>PII redaction and guardrail hooks before and after the call; policies on what gets logged\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Observability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenTelemetry traces, logs to Langfuse, Datadog and similar tools, per-request cost\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Change management\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model aliases (\u003ccode>chat-default\u003c/code> points at a real model), weighted canary rollouts of a new model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Model aliases are underrated. If applications ask for \u003ccode>support-answer\u003c/code> rather than a provider's model id, moving to a new model is a gateway config change plus an eval run, not a redeploy of every service.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Options (as of late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Open source, self-hosted\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LiteLLM proxy, Portkey gateway, Envoy AI Gateway, Kong AI Gateway\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Control, data stays in your network\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Managed service\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cloudflare AI Gateway, Vercel AI Gateway\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast start with no servers: analytics, fallbacks, spend controls (and caching on Cloudflare)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hosted router and marketplace\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenRouter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One bill for hundreds of models, provider routing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cloud platform\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Azure API Management AI gateway, Amazon Bedrock (including cross-region inference), Google Vertex AI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Enterprises standardised on one cloud\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A minimal LiteLLM proxy setup\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">yaml\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>model_list:\n  - model_name: atlas-small\n    litellm_params: {model: openai/&lt;small-model&gt;, api_key: os.environ/OPENAI_API_KEY}\n  - model_name: atlas-large                  # two deployments with one name are load-balanced\n    litellm_params: {model: anthropic/&lt;large-model&gt;, api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}\n  - model_name: atlas-large\n    litellm_params: {model: bedrock/&lt;large-model-id&gt;, aws_region_name: eu-west-1}\nlitellm_settings:\n  num_retries: 2\n  request_timeout: 60\n  fallbacks: [{\"atlas-large\": [\"atlas-small\"]}]\n  cache: true\n  cache_params: {type: redis}\nrouter_settings:\n  routing_strategy: simple-shuffle\n  allowed_fails: 3\n  cooldown_time: 30\ngeneral_settings:\n  master_key: os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Start it with \u003ccode>litellm --config config.yaml\u003c/code> (port 4000 by default), point any OpenAI SDK at \u003ccode>http://localhost:4000\u003c/code>, and issue a key with a budget:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>curl -X POST http://localhost:4000/key/generate \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"team_id\": \"support-bot\", \"max_budget\": 200, \"budget_duration\": \"30d\", \"rpm_limit\": 600}'\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Running a gateway well\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>It needs state.\u003c/b> LiteLLM keys, spend and budgets live in Postgres (set \u003ccode>DATABASE_URL\u003c/code>). Its docs warn that without a database, budgets cannot be enforced and the global budget fails open: requests keep flowing past the limit. With more than one instance, add Redis so rate-limit counters, router state and the response cache are shared; otherwise each instance enforces limits independently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It is a new single point of failure\u003c/b> and an extra network hop. Run at least two replicas behind a load balancer, add health checks, and measure the latency it adds (aim for a few milliseconds).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Translation can lose features.\u003c/b> Provider-specific options (cache breakpoints, thinking settings, some tool types) may be dropped or mapped imperfectly. Test the features you depend on through the gateway, and use passthrough routes where needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It sees every prompt.\u003c/b> Decide what is logged, for how long, who can read it, and redact PII before storage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Own the config like code.\u003c/b> Keep it in git, review changes, and run the eval suite when routes or models change.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Build or buy\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A thin wrapper module in your application is fine for one app and one or two providers. Once several teams or services call models, or you need per-team budgets and audit logs, a shared gateway pays for itself. Managed gateways are fastest to adopt; self-hosted ones keep data and control inside your network.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does an LLM gateway give you that a client library in each application does not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Central enforcement and visibility: per-team keys and budgets, rate limits, routing, retries, fallbacks and caching configured once; consistent logs, traces and cost attribution across all apps; model aliases so a model switch is one config change; and a single place for guardrails, redaction and logging policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why would you register two deployments under the same model name in a gateway?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The gateway load-balances between them, which adds capacity (two quotas), resilience (one region or provider can fail) and lets you spread traffic across clouds while applications keep calling one alias.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two new risks a gateway introduces and how to mitigate them.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is a single point of failure and an extra hop: run several replicas with health checks and measure added latency. It sees every prompt: restrict access, set retention and redact sensitive data in logs. Another: budgets silently not enforced if the state store is missing: run it with its database and Redis as documented.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:reliability/capacity-planning-rate-limits-quotas-and-load-te":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Capacity planning answers one question before launch: at peak, how many requests, tokens and simultaneous streams will we send, and will the provider let us? \u003cb>Little's law\u003c/b> gives concurrency, simple multiplication gives tokens per minute, and a \u003cb>load test\u003c/b> with production-shaped traffic shows where latency and errors break. Higher tiers, more deployments, priority tiers and provisioned throughput are different ways to buy headroom.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>From traffic to numbers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Example peak: 20 requests per second, each with 4,000 input tokens and 400 output tokens, taking 8 seconds end to end.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>requests per minute   = 20 × 60              = 1,200 RPM\ninput tokens / minute = 1,200 × 4,000        = 4.8 million\noutput tokens / minute= 1,200 × 400          = 480,000\nconcurrency (Little's law: L = arrival rate × time in system) = 20 × 8 = 160 open streams\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Concurrency sizes your own system: HTTP connection pools, worker counts, gateway replicas and file descriptors must handle at least 160 open streams. Add headroom for bursts and retries; planning for 1.5 to 2 times the expected peak is common.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prompt caching changes the quota picture on some providers. Anthropic counts only uncached input toward input-tokens-per-minute (ITPM) for most current models, so at a 75% cache hit rate the 4.8 million input tokens use only 1.2 million of ITPM. Check your provider's rules.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How provider limits work\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dimensions\u003c/b>: requests per minute (RPM) and tokens per minute (TPM), sometimes split into input and output (Anthropic uses RPM, ITPM and OTPM), and sometimes daily limits. They apply per model or model family, per organisation or project.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Token bucket\u003c/b>: Anthropic documents that capacity refills continuously up to the maximum rather than resetting each minute, so a burst can drain it and then you are limited to the refill rate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tiers rise over time\u003c/b>: OpenAI moves organisations up usage tiers as cumulative credit purchases grow; Anthropic assigns tiers from usage history and account standing. You can also request increases. Anthropic tiers also carry a \u003cb>monthly spend cap\u003c/b>: reach it and requests return 429 until the next month, and retrying does not help. Know your cap before a launch.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Estimation before the call\u003c/b>: OpenAI counts the larger of \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> and an estimate from your input toward the limit when the request arrives, so an oversized \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> wastes quota. Anthropic counts output tokens as they are generated, so \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> does not affect its output limit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ramp limits\u003c/b>: sudden growth can be throttled even below your limits. OpenAI returns 429 \u003ccode>slow_down\u003c/code> and suggests, once above 1 million TPM, growing by no more than 50% every 15 minutes; Anthropic describes similar acceleration limits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Headers\u003c/b> tell you where you stand: \u003ccode>x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens\u003c/code> (OpenAI), \u003ccode>anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining\u003c/code> and similar, plus \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> on 429s. Log them and alert before you hit zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Buying headroom\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>What you get\u003c/th>\u003cth>When it pays\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Higher tier or limit increase\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bigger limits, same price\u003c/td>\u003ctd>First step; ask early, approval can take days\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>More deployments behind the gateway\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Several keys, regions or clouds add up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cheap, adds operational complexity\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Priority or fast tier\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Faster, steadier latency for a higher per-token price\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Latency-critical peaks\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Provisioned or reserved throughput\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dedicated capacity billed per hour or month (Azure OpenAI provisioned throughput units, Amazon Bedrock and Vertex AI provisioned throughput, enterprise reserved tiers)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High, steady volume needing predictable latency\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Self-hosting open models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your own GPUs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>See the Serving module\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Reserved capacity is paid for whether you use it or not, so \u003cb>utilisation decides\u003c/b>. If a unit's price works out at 60% of pay-as-you-go per token when fully used, it only becomes cheaper above 60% average utilisation; at 30% utilisation you pay twice the list price per token. Typical pattern: reserve for the steady baseline, burst to pay-as-you-go above it. Anthropic notes that its Priority Tier commitments are closed to new buyers as of late 2026, so check what each provider currently sells.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Load testing\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shape the traffic like production\u003c/b>: real distributions of input and output length (sample from logs), streaming on, the same prompt prefixes (so caching behaves as in production), and Poisson-style bursty arrivals rather than a steady drip.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ramp in steps\u003c/b> (for example 2, 5, 10, 20, 30 requests per second), holding each long enough to stabilise. Record TTFT and total latency at p50 and p95, error rates by status code, and tokens per minute. The \u003cb>knee\u003c/b>, where p95 shoots up or 429s appear, is your real capacity.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise the failure paths\u003c/b>: what happens at 429? Requests should queue briefly, shed load with a friendly message, or degrade to a smaller model, never pile up retries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Watch the bill.\u003c/b> A load test against a paid API costs real money; use a mock server or a cheap model to test your own infrastructure, then a short, capped run against the real model.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Tools: k6 and Locust for general HTTP load; \u003ccode>vllm bench serve\u003c/code> (it accepts \u003ccode>--base-url\u003c/code> and an \u003ccode>openai-chat\u003c/code> backend, so it works against any OpenAI-compatible endpoint) and GuideLLM for LLM-specific metrics such as TTFT and inter-token latency.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Admission control completes the picture: put a queue with a maximum wait in front of the model, give paying or interactive traffic priority over background jobs, and reject early with a clear message rather than timing out late.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Peak traffic is 10 requests per second, each with 6,000 input and 500 output tokens, taking 12 seconds. What concurrency, input TPM and output TPM do you need to plan for?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Concurrency is 10 × 12 = 120 open requests by Little's law. Requests per minute are 600, so input is 600 × 6,000 = 3.6 million tokens per minute and output is 600 × 500 = 300,000 tokens per minute, before adding 1.5 to 2 times headroom for bursts and retries.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should you set \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> close to the real output length on OpenAI?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>OpenAI counts the larger of \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> and the estimated tokens toward your rate limit when the request arrives, so a needlessly large \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> consumes quota you never use and makes you hit 429s earlier.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When does provisioned throughput pay off?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When traffic is high and steady enough that average utilisation of the reserved capacity exceeds the ratio of its effective per-token price to the pay-as-you-go price, or when you need predictable latency that shared capacity cannot give. For spiky or small traffic, idle reserved capacity makes each token more expensive.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:reliability/a-semantic-cache-measured":"\u003cp>You will build the core of a semantic cache in about 30 lines, then do what most teams skip: measure how often it returns the wrong answer. The output is a table that tells you which threshold, if any, is safe for your traffic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11 or newer: \u003ccode>pip install openai numpy\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>An embedding model behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Locally: install Ollama and run \u003ccode>ollama pull nomic-embed-text\u003c/code>. Or use any provider's embedding model with its base URL and key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>20 minutes to write question pairs from a domain you know (your team's docs or the Atlas corpus).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Create \u003ccode>pairs.jsonl\u003c/code>. Each line pairs a question already in the cache with a new incoming question, and says whether the cached answer would be correct for it. Aim for 40 pairs: 20 paraphrases (\u003ccode>\"same\": true\u003c/code>) and 20 near-misses that share words but need a different answer (\u003ccode>\"same\": false\u003c/code>). Start from these:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"cached\": \"How do I reset my password?\", \"new\": \"I forgot my password, how can I change it?\", \"same\": true}\n{\"cached\": \"How do I reset my password?\", \"new\": \"How do I reset my 2FA device?\", \"same\": false}\n{\"cached\": \"What are your opening hours?\", \"new\": \"When are you open?\", \"same\": true}\n{\"cached\": \"Can I get a refund for a damaged item?\", \"new\": \"Can I get a refund for a late delivery?\", \"same\": false}\n{\"cached\": \"How do I export my data to CSV?\", \"new\": \"Is there a way to download my data as a CSV file?\", \"same\": true}\n{\"cached\": \"How do I export my data to CSV?\", \"new\": \"How do I import data from a CSV?\", \"same\": false}\n{\"cached\": \"Do you ship to Canada?\", \"new\": \"Can you deliver to Canada?\", \"same\": true}\n{\"cached\": \"Do you ship to Canada?\", \"new\": \"Do you ship to Mexico?\", \"same\": false}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Write the embedding function and the cache in \u003ccode>semcache.py\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nimport numpy as np\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")\nEMBED_MODEL = \"nomic-embed-text\"\n\ndef embed(texts: list[str]) -&gt; np.ndarray:\n    r = client.embeddings.create(model=EMBED_MODEL, input=texts)\n    v = np.array([d.embedding for d in r.data])\n    return v / np.linalg.norm(v, axis=1, keepdims=True)    # unit length: dot product = cosine\n\nclass SemanticCache:\n    def __init__(self, threshold: float = 0.9):\n        self.threshold, self.keys, self.answers = threshold, None, []\n\n    def lookup(self, question: str):\n        if self.keys is None:\n            return None, 0.0\n        sims = self.keys @ embed([question])[0]\n        i = int(np.argmax(sims))\n        return (self.answers[i] if sims[i] &gt;= self.threshold else None), float(sims[i])\n\n    def add(self, question: str, answer: str):\n        v = embed([question])\n        self.keys = v if self.keys is None else np.vstack([self.keys, v])\n        self.answers.append(answer)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Measure every pair's similarity once, then sweep thresholds. Add to the same file:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>pairs = [json.loads(line) for line in open(\"pairs.jsonl\")]\na = embed([p[\"cached\"] for p in pairs])\nb = embed([p[\"new\"] for p in pairs])\nsims = np.sum(a * b, axis=1)\nsame = np.array([p[\"same\"] for p in pairs])\n\nprint(\"threshold  paraphrases caught  wrong hits  precision of hits\")\nfor t in (0.70, 0.75, 0.80, 0.85, 0.90, 0.95):\n    hit = sims &gt;= t\n    good, bad = int(np.sum(hit &amp; same)), int(np.sum(hit &amp; ~same))\n    print(f\"{t:9.2f}  {good / same.sum():18.0%}  {bad:10d}  {good / max(1, good + bad):17.0%}\")\n\nfor p, s in sorted(zip(pairs, sims), key=lambda x: -x[1])[:10]:\n    print(f\"{s:.3f} same={p['same']!s:5} {p['new']}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Read the sorted list. Find the highest-scoring near-miss: that score is the lowest threshold at which you can avoid all wrong hits on this set. Note how many paraphrases you would still catch at that threshold.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Compare with an exact cache. Count how many \u003ccode>new\u003c/code> questions are identical to their \u003ccode>cached\u003c/code> question after lower-casing and stripping punctuation. Usually none: exact caching catches retries and repeated jobs, not paraphrases.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Turn it into money and risk. For a feature with 100,000 questions a day at $0.0055 per model call, assume the share of traffic that looks like your paraphrases. Compute the daily saving at your chosen threshold, and the expected wrong answers per day from your measured precision.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"7\">\u003cli>Try the multi-turn trap. Embed the follow-up \"What about business accounts?\" asked after two different first questions (about pricing, and about data retention). The last message is identical, so a cache keyed on it alone returns the first conversation's answer to the second. Write down how you would prevent this (key on the full conversation, or disable the semantic cache for multi-turn requests).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"8\">\u003cli>Optional: switch \u003ccode>EMBED_MODEL\u003c/code> to a second embedding model and rerun step 3. Note how the whole similarity scale shifts.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Paraphrases typically score high, but some near-misses (\"ship to Canada\" versus \"ship to Mexico\") score nearly as high, because they differ in one decisive word.\u003c/li>\u003cli>High thresholds give few or no wrong hits but catch only a fraction of paraphrases; lower thresholds catch more and start returning wrong answers. There is rarely a threshold that is both safe and catches most paraphrases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Thresholds do not transfer between embedding models: 0.85 can be strict for one model and loose for another.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The multi-turn case fails at any threshold if the key ignores conversation context.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your measurements show 95% precision at a threshold that catches 60% of paraphrases. Is that good enough to ship?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It depends on the cost of a wrong answer and the volume. At 5% of semantic hits being wrong, a feature that serves 10,000 semantic hits a day gives 500 wrong answers a day. For low-stakes FAQ content with a visible \"not what you asked?\" link that may be acceptable; for support, billing, legal or medical answers it usually is not, and a higher threshold, human review of cached answers, or no semantic cache is safer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the threshold be re-measured when you change embedding model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each model produces its own similarity distribution, so the same cosine value means different things; a threshold tuned on one model can be far too loose or too strict on another.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:cost-optimised-llm-gateway":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Starting point: Atlas after M13. It has a provider-agnostic client with logs of tokens, cost and latency (M1), a cost and cache meter (M2), versioned prompts and a regression suite (M3), typed tools (M4), a context assembler (M5), memory (M6), hybrid search (M7), grounded answers with citations (M8), a graph with human approval for actions (M9), an MCP server (M10), an eval suite in CI (M11), tracing and dashboards (M12) and red-team hardening (M13).\u003c/p>\u003cp>M14 puts a gateway between Atlas and every model. Application code stops naming providers and models; it asks for aliases such as \u003ccode>atlas-small\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>atlas-large\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>atlas-embed\u003c/code>. The gateway decides where each request goes, caches what is safe to cache, survives a provider outage, and enforces budgets. You finish with evidence: the same eval score as M13 at a lower cost per task and with better availability.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One gateway for all model calls: LiteLLM proxy, another gateway, or your own \u003ccode>atlas/gateway.py\u003c/code>. Atlas code uses only aliases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Routing\u003c/b>: a router or cascade sends simple questions to the small model and complex ones (multi-step, account actions) to the large model, with escalation when a check fails (no citation, invalid tool arguments).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Caching\u003c/b>: provider prompt caching confirmed from usage fields (cached tokens above zero on repeat turns); an exact response cache for deterministic calls such as query rewriting and embeddings; a semantic cache only if your lab measurements justify it, restricted to anonymous single-turn questions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reliability\u003c/b>: per-phase timeouts, retries in exactly one layer with backoff and jitter, a fallback chain across two providers, and a circuit breaker.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Budgets\u003c/b>: per-task caps on steps, tokens, dollars and time in the Atlas graph; a per-user daily budget at the gateway; graceful wrap-up when a cap is hit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Telemetry\u003c/b>: every request logs route, model, cache status, tokens, cost, latency and whether a fallback was used, visible on your M12 dashboard.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Skeleton\u003c/b> (1 h): run the gateway, define aliases, switch Atlas to call it, and confirm traces and costs still appear in your dashboard.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reliability\u003c/b> (1.5 h): timeouts, retries, fallbacks and a circuit breaker. Build a fault injector: a tiny local server that returns 503 for a set fraction of requests or stalls before the first token, and register it as a deployment.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Routing\u003c/b> (1.5 h): implement the router or cascade; run the M11 eval suite per route; calibrate the threshold so quality on small-model traffic meets your bar.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Caching\u003c/b> (1 h): add the exact cache with hit and miss counters; verify prompt-cache hits in usage fields; add the semantic cache only behind a flag with the threshold from the lab.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Budgets\u003c/b> (1 h): add the per-task budget object and per-user gateway budgets; write a test that forces a loop and checks Atlas stops cleanly with a progress summary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evidence\u003c/b> (1.5 h): run the eval suite and a 15-minute load test with production-shaped traffic against M13 (direct calls) and M14 (gateway). Kill the primary provider halfway through the load test.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One command (\u003ccode>make compare\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>python -m atlas.compare\u003c/code>) runs the eval suite and load test for both configurations and writes a report table: eval score, cost per task, p50 and p95 TTFT and total latency, error rate, cache hit rates and escalation rate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The M14 eval score is within noise of M13 (state the method, for example a paired comparison on the same cases) and cost per task is lower.\u003c/li>\u003cli>During the provider kill, user-visible errors stay under your target and the logs show fallbacks being used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A forced runaway task stops at its budget with a useful message, and the budget hit is visible in traces.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Hedged requests on the answer path when no first token arrives by the p95 TTFT.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Route nightly re-indexing and eval runs through the Batch API or flex tier and report the saving.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Serve the small model yourself (the Serving module) behind the same \u003ccode>atlas-small\u003c/code> alias and compare cost and latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Weighted canary: send 5% of traffic to a new model version behind an alias and compare live metrics.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>With LiteLLM, two deployments under one \u003ccode>model_name\u003c/code> are load-balanced automatically; \u003ccode>fallbacks\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>num_retries\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>allowed_fails\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>cooldown_time\u003c/code> cover most of the reliability milestone.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Give your budgets and spend tracking a database from day one; budget enforcement without one may silently do nothing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep the eval suite deterministic where possible (temperature 0, fixed retrieval) so the M13 versus M14 difference is the gateway, not noise.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
